Q.2.Experiment: Shell Script to Check Pass or Fail Using If-Else

1. Aim:-
To write and execute a shell script that accepts the marks of a student and displays whether the student has passed or failed using an if-else statement.

2. Theory:-
A shell script is a file containing a series of commands executed by a shell interpreter. In Linux, the Bash shell is commonly used to execute shell scripts.
The if-else statement is a conditional statement used to make decisions based on a given condition.
If the student's marks are 40 or above, the student is declared Passed.
If the student's marks are below 40, the student is declared Failed.
The read command accepts input from the user, and the echo command displays the output.

3. Command Explanation:-
#!/bin/bash — Specifies the Bash shell interpreter.
echo — Displays a message on the screen.
read marks — Accepts the student's marks and stores them in the variable marks.
if — Checks whether the given condition is true.
[ "$marks" -ge 40 ] — Checks whether the marks are greater than or equal to 40. The -ge operator means greater than or equal to.
then — Executes the commands when the condition is true.
else — Executes the commands when the condition is false.
fi — Marks the end of the if-else statement.
chmod +x result.sh — Gives execute permission to the script.
./result.sh — Executes the shell script.

4. Program:-
#!/bin/bash

echo "Enter the student's marks:"
read marks

if [ "$marks" -ge 40 ]
then
    echo "Student has Passed."
else
    echo "Student has Failed."
fi
